WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:01.610
- Trading platform Robinhood

00:00:01.610 --> 00:00:03.670
- And one of the most highly
anticipated IPOs of the year

00:00:03.670 --> 00:00:06.190
- That's a big deal
for the retail investor

00:00:06.190 --> 00:00:08.610
but it could also come
with some big risks.

00:00:08.610 --> 00:00:11.330
- [Narrator] Last year,
millions of investors traded

00:00:11.330 --> 00:00:13.180
on the brokerage app Robinhood.

00:00:13.180 --> 00:00:14.900
They were part of a trend.

00:00:14.900 --> 00:00:17.920
Since 2020, individual
investors bought billions

00:00:17.920 --> 00:00:20.300
of dollars in equities, driven by the rise

00:00:20.300 --> 00:00:23.180
in no-fee trading apps with
Robinhood leading the way.

00:00:23.180 --> 00:00:24.950
- Robinhood, when it came onto the scene,

00:00:24.950 --> 00:00:26.850
really upended the industry.

00:00:26.850 --> 00:00:29.930
- We believe that the more
people that have access

00:00:29.930 --> 00:00:32.420
to the markets and can
start investing earlier

00:00:32.420 --> 00:00:34.690
the better off our economy will be.

00:00:34.690 --> 00:00:36.580
- [Narrator] After a
year of dramatic growth,

00:00:36.580 --> 00:00:39.680
Robinhood filed for its IPO on July 1st.

00:00:39.680 --> 00:00:42.020
But the filing comes
as the brokerage faces

00:00:42.020 --> 00:00:44.840
a collision course with
regulators, including a new

00:00:44.840 --> 00:00:47.000
Securities and Exchange
Commission chairman

00:00:47.000 --> 00:00:49.610
over a controversial
practice that Robinhood uses

00:00:49.610 --> 00:00:51.480
to generate most of its revenue.

00:00:51.480 --> 00:00:54.700
Here's how Robinhood
went from startup to IPO

00:00:54.700 --> 00:00:57.300
in less than a decade and why it's facing

00:00:57.300 --> 00:00:59.883
so much turmoil as it
gears up to go public.

00:01:01.950 --> 00:01:05.490
In the '90s, brokerages
like E*Trade and Ameritrade

00:01:05.490 --> 00:01:09.000
started offering online trading
services which helped open

00:01:09.000 --> 00:01:11.350
up the stock market to retail investors.

00:01:11.350 --> 00:01:12.830
But it was expensive.

00:01:12.830 --> 00:01:15.640
- [Spokesman] Internet trade
for $8, Touch-Tone trade...

00:01:15.640 --> 00:01:17.430
- [Narrator] Trading fees
could add up to hundreds

00:01:17.430 --> 00:01:19.510
or even thousands of dollars a year.

00:01:19.510 --> 00:01:21.010
- That was one of the reason why we saw

00:01:21.010 --> 00:01:23.480
relatively muted levels
of individual investors

00:01:23.480 --> 00:01:24.350
in the market.

00:01:24.350 --> 00:01:26.180
- [Narrator] Caitlin
McCabe reports on Robinhood

00:01:26.180 --> 00:01:28.290
and retail investing for
the Wall Street Journal.

00:01:28.290 --> 00:01:31.650
- It was cost prohibitive
to try to be trading

00:01:31.650 --> 00:01:33.990
all the time with these fees.

00:01:33.990 --> 00:01:36.190
- [Narrator] That was the case until 2015

00:01:36.190 --> 00:01:38.590
when Robinhood released its trading app.

00:01:38.590 --> 00:01:40.820
- From the very beginning,
our mission has been

00:01:40.820 --> 00:01:42.940
to democratize the financial system.

00:01:42.940 --> 00:01:45.500
- [Narrator] This is Robinhood
co-founder, Baiju Bhatt.

00:01:45.500 --> 00:01:49.360
- And for us, this isn't a
headline, it's not a gimmick

00:01:49.360 --> 00:01:52.020
or a billboard ad, it's the central reason

00:01:52.020 --> 00:01:53.370
why we started the company.

00:01:53.370 --> 00:01:55.820
- [Narrator] To attract new
investors, Robinhood offered

00:01:55.820 --> 00:01:58.660
features that made
investing more accessible.

00:01:58.660 --> 00:02:00.510
It was one of the earliest brokerages

00:02:00.510 --> 00:02:02.310
to offer no-fee trading.

00:02:02.310 --> 00:02:05.660
It was also a pioneer in
fractional share trading.

00:02:05.660 --> 00:02:09.370
- So suddenly, you could buy
$10 of Amazon or $50 of Amazon.

00:02:09.370 --> 00:02:11.890
You could just buy, basically,
a slice of that stock

00:02:11.890 --> 00:02:14.500
and it just made it much
easier for investors

00:02:14.500 --> 00:02:16.070
to access the market in that way.

00:02:16.070 --> 00:02:17.990
- [Narrator] But price
wasn't the only thing

00:02:17.990 --> 00:02:19.570
keeping retail investors
out of the market.

00:02:19.570 --> 00:02:22.950
- So it was just really
intimidating to try to trade

00:02:22.950 --> 00:02:25.050
and to try to figure out charts

00:02:25.050 --> 00:02:27.540
and to understand what was
happening in the market.

00:02:27.540 --> 00:02:29.820
- [Narrator] Robinhood also
experimented with features

00:02:29.820 --> 00:02:31.890
aimed at keeping people engaged.

00:02:31.890 --> 00:02:33.670
When users would log on to the app

00:02:33.670 --> 00:02:37.140
and place their first trade,
it used to rain confetti,

00:02:37.140 --> 00:02:39.680
stock numbers turned like slot machines,

00:02:39.680 --> 00:02:43.290
and some of its messages
to investors used emojis.

00:02:43.290 --> 00:02:46.020
- And it was just a very
inviting app, it was very fun.

00:02:46.020 --> 00:02:48.700
They were just very
kind of on the forefront

00:02:48.700 --> 00:02:52.830
of trying to invite that
Millennial crowd into trading.

00:02:52.830 --> 00:02:54.840
And I think it worked, I think
that's part of the reason

00:02:54.840 --> 00:02:57.170
why Millennials liked the app.

00:02:57.170 --> 00:02:59.190
- [Narrator] Robinhood's
user base grew quickly

00:02:59.190 --> 00:03:01.750
which caught the attention
of its competition.

00:03:01.750 --> 00:03:05.100
By 2019, major brokerages
like Charles Schwab

00:03:05.100 --> 00:03:06.890
and TD Ameritrade had adopted

00:03:06.890 --> 00:03:08.930
zero commission trading, as well.

00:03:08.930 --> 00:03:10.900
- It was just recognition of kind of what

00:03:10.900 --> 00:03:13.550
Robinhood had built and
the market share that

00:03:13.550 --> 00:03:16.500
it had amassed and that
kind of was the genesis

00:03:16.500 --> 00:03:19.040
of this new trading era that we see today.

00:03:19.040 --> 00:03:22.490
- [Narrator] And then in early
2020, the COVID-19 pandemic

00:03:22.490 --> 00:03:23.950
took hold of the world.

00:03:23.950 --> 00:03:27.390
- We are looking at another
day of extreme volatility.

00:03:27.390 --> 00:03:29.480
Stocks opened just moments ago

00:03:29.480 --> 00:03:32.210
with the DOW down more than 600 point.

00:03:32.210 --> 00:03:34.920
- I think with the market
volatility, a lot of people said

00:03:34.920 --> 00:03:36.300
I think this is a good time for me

00:03:36.300 --> 00:03:37.650
to try to get into the market

00:03:37.650 --> 00:03:40.310
and that's where we've
really seen this explosion

00:03:40.310 --> 00:03:43.260
of activity from individual
investors ever since.

00:03:43.260 --> 00:03:45.110
- [Narrator] According to its IPO filing,

00:03:45.110 --> 00:03:48.420
Robinhood counted 18 million
users with funded accounts

00:03:48.420 --> 00:03:51.490
at the end of March
2021 and the brokerage's

00:03:51.490 --> 00:03:55.983
first quarter revenue more than
quadrupled to $522 million.

00:03:57.130 --> 00:04:00.440
But as Robinhood has grown,
so has criticism that

00:04:00.440 --> 00:04:03.210
some of the app's features,
the very ones that help

00:04:03.210 --> 00:04:05.580
make it successful could cause problems

00:04:05.580 --> 00:04:07.620
for inexperienced investors.

00:04:07.620 --> 00:04:10.420
Critics said the app's
interface which made it fun

00:04:10.420 --> 00:04:13.170
and easy to use actually gamified trading

00:04:13.170 --> 00:04:15.770
and put inexperienced investors at risk.

00:04:15.770 --> 00:04:17.900
- Trading's a challenging business.

00:04:17.900 --> 00:04:20.340
- [Narrator] Larry Tabb is
a financial markets analyst

00:04:20.340 --> 00:04:23.350
who has studied market
structure for over 25 years.

00:04:23.350 --> 00:04:26.690
- You're competing not only with some

00:04:26.690 --> 00:04:28.530
of the largest investors in the world,

00:04:28.530 --> 00:04:31.770
but you're trading with some of the guys

00:04:31.770 --> 00:04:34.270
with the biggest computers
and the fastest data feeds

00:04:34.270 --> 00:04:36.430
and the most sophisticated analytics.

00:04:36.430 --> 00:04:38.710
It's really difficult
to continually beat them

00:04:38.710 --> 00:04:39.710
day in and day out.

00:04:39.710 --> 00:04:41.610
- [Narrator] Over the
past year, Robinhood has

00:04:41.610 --> 00:04:44.230
also faced criticism over
how it makes its money

00:04:44.230 --> 00:04:47.440
and how it previously
communicated that with investors.

00:04:47.440 --> 00:04:51.940
In December 2020, Robinhood
agreed to pay $65 million

00:04:51.940 --> 00:04:54.440
to settle claims that it
didn't sufficiently disclose

00:04:54.440 --> 00:04:57.320
its business deals with
high-speed trading firms.

00:04:57.320 --> 00:04:59.020
And then there was GameStop.

00:04:59.020 --> 00:05:02.650
- Outrage erupting in this wild
and expensive battle that's

00:05:02.650 --> 00:05:05.020
playing out between Wall
Street heavyweights,

00:05:05.020 --> 00:05:08.280
small investors, and these
popular stock trading apps.

00:05:08.280 --> 00:05:11.990
- [Narrator] In January 2021,
Robinhood came under scrutiny

00:05:11.990 --> 00:05:14.830
after it restricted users
from buying GameStop

00:05:14.830 --> 00:05:17.040
as the company's share price surged.

00:05:17.040 --> 00:05:19.170
The trading blackout caused some investors

00:05:19.170 --> 00:05:21.210
to lose out on potential gains.

00:05:21.210 --> 00:05:23.770
About 50 class-action lawsuits,

00:05:23.770 --> 00:05:26.030
investigations by regulators,

00:05:26.030 --> 00:05:29.070
and multiple hearings with
members of Congress followed.

00:05:29.070 --> 00:05:32.100
Here's Robinhood CEO speaking
with the House Committee

00:05:32.100 --> 00:05:33.510
on financial services.

00:05:33.510 --> 00:05:36.670
- Despite the unprecedented
market conditions in January,

00:05:36.670 --> 00:05:40.300
at the end of the day, what
happened is unacceptable to us.

00:05:40.300 --> 00:05:43.460
To our customers, I'm
sorry and I apologize.

00:05:43.460 --> 00:05:46.060
- [Narrator] During the
hearings, lawmakers scrutinized

00:05:46.060 --> 00:05:48.300
Robinhood's use of a
financial practice called

00:05:48.300 --> 00:05:50.940
payment for order flow
which allows the brokerage

00:05:50.940 --> 00:05:54.010
to route customer orders through
high-speed trading firms.

00:05:54.010 --> 00:05:56.810
According to Robinhood's
IPO filing, the company made

00:05:56.810 --> 00:06:00.510
around 81% of its first quarter
revenue from this practice.

00:06:00.510 --> 00:06:03.060
Payment for order flow
has raised red flags

00:06:03.060 --> 00:06:05.690
with the SEC's new Chairman, Gary Gensler.

00:06:05.690 --> 00:06:08.060
- This inherent conflict is there

00:06:08.060 --> 00:06:12.760
and whether we can address
it enough through disclosure

00:06:12.760 --> 00:06:15.970
or it sort of implicates the
broader market structure.

00:06:15.970 --> 00:06:17.620
And I've asked staff to think about that

00:06:17.620 --> 00:06:19.350
broader market structure.

00:06:19.350 --> 00:06:21.710
- [Narrator] Gensler is
reviewing payment for order flow

00:06:21.710 --> 00:06:24.380
which could have implications
for the future of Robinhood

00:06:24.380 --> 00:06:26.150
and no-fee trading, in general.

00:06:26.150 --> 00:06:28.660
- This is part and parcel of Robinhood

00:06:28.660 --> 00:06:30.880
how they make money, so it
will be really interesting

00:06:30.880 --> 00:06:35.880
to see if Gensler decides to
be a one-man wrecking crew

00:06:36.030 --> 00:06:40.440
and really radically
reshift how the economics

00:06:40.440 --> 00:06:42.270
of the retail brokerage business.

00:06:42.270 --> 00:06:43.160
- [Narrator] Robinhood has faced

00:06:43.160 --> 00:06:45.100
other regulatory issues, as well.

00:06:45.100 --> 00:06:49.000
In June, the brokerage agreed
to pay nearly $70 million

00:06:49.000 --> 00:06:51.720
to resolve allegations
that it misled customers,

00:06:51.720 --> 00:06:54.360
approved ineligible traders
for risky strategies,

00:06:54.360 --> 00:06:56.940
and didn't supervise
technology that failed

00:06:56.940 --> 00:06:58.840
and locked millions out of trading.

00:06:58.840 --> 00:07:01.740
According to its IPO
filing, Robinhood continues

00:07:01.740 --> 00:07:04.220
to face a number of regulatory inquiries.

00:07:04.220 --> 00:07:07.360
Still, some industry veterans
say Robinhood will survive

00:07:07.360 --> 00:07:11.615
the SEC's review even if
regulations are imposed.

00:07:11.615 --> 00:07:14.948
(soft electronic music)

